00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:13.980
And they fall into this, not quite romance, but certainly they're intrigued by each other, they start writing letters to each other.
他们陷入了一种说不上是浪漫，但彼此确实充满吸引力的关系，他们开始给对方写信。

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:25.919
And I kept trying to write something that was just a little more edging them into the physical, and something in me was like, ugh, like something wouldn't let me do it.
我一直试图写一些稍微能把他们推向身体接触的情节，但我的内心深处有个声音在说，呃，某种东西就是不让我这么做。

00:09:25.919 --> 00:09:35.399
And paying attention to the part of me that wouldn't let that happen was what led to the third act of the movie, which I think is really right.
而正是去关注我内心那个不允许这种情况发生的部分，才促成了电影的第三幕，我觉得这真的很对。

00:09:35.399 --> 00:09:41.639
And there was something in me that was keeping, and it wasn't like I wanted to write a full blown romance.
我内心有什么东西在克制着，而且并不是说我不想写一段轰轰烈烈的浪漫史。

00:09:41.639 --> 00:09:45.840
It wouldn't even let me do like the smallest kind of stuff.
它甚至连最微小的那些举动都不让我做。

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:47.159
So I really had to honor that.
所以我真的必须顺从这一点。

00:09:47.159 --> 00:10:05.120
And I think it's, when you talk to writers in fictional spaces, it's most of them I think, I don't want to speak, but a lot of them will say, yeah, the characters took over at a certain point, and that's when writing becomes really fun, because you feel like you're being used by something bigger than your own conscious mind.
我想，当你与虚构创作领域的作家交流时，他们大多数人——我不想替所有人说话，但很多人会说：“是的，到了某个阶段角色就接管了一切。”这就是写作变得真正有趣的时候，因为你会觉得自己被某种比你自己的清醒意识更宏大的东西所驱使。

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:14.200
And I wrote you this little day to sing to you in New York City.
我为你写了这首小曲，要在纽约市唱给你听。

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:23.159
Hi, I'm Alex Goldman, host of the Hyper-Fixed Podcast.
嗨，我是亚历克斯·戈德曼，播客《超级修复》的主持人。

00:10:23.159 --> 00:10:26.840
Each week, we take listeners' problems and try to solve them for them.
每周，我们都会收集听众的问题并试着为他们解决。

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:29.557
like, I'm 30 and I'm scared to drive in New York.
比如，“我30岁了，但很害怕在纽约开车。”

00:10:29.557 --> 00:10:33.493
Or, why can't I adjust the volume of my car stereo when I'm in reverse?
或者，“为什么我倒车时不能调节车载音响的音量？”

00:10:33.493 --> 00:10:35.656
We also solve non-car related problems.
我们也解决与汽车无关的问题。

00:10:35.656 --> 00:10:42.532
If you have a problem, not only will we fix it, we'll expose the hidden systems that caused that problem in the first place.
如果你遇到了问题，我们不仅会帮你解决，还会揭示导致该问题的隐藏系统。

00:10:42.532 --> 00:10:45.138
That's the Hyper-Fixed Podcast from Radiotopia.
这就是来自Radiotopia的《超级修复》播客。

00:10:45.138 --> 00:10:48.409
Find it wherever you find podcasts or at HyperFixedPod.com.
你可以在任何收听播客的地方找到它，或者访问 HyperFixedPod.com。

00:10:48.409 --> 00:11:17.139
I read a quote from Hemingway recently where we were talking about Old Man and the Sea, And it was just this very like prickly quote, because everyone tried to interpret the way, he goes, this old fisherman has nothing, but he goes out and he catches this huge fish.
我最近读到海明威的一段话，当时我们在谈论《老人与海》，那段话非常尖锐，因为每个人都试图解读他的意图，他说，这个老渔夫一无所有，但他出海捕到了一条巨大的鱼。

00:11:17.139 --> 00:11:20.419
And then he's got to get this huge fish back to land.
然后他得把这条大鱼带回岸上。

00:11:20.419 --> 00:11:23.740
And the fish is picked apart as he goes by other fish.
在回去的路上，鱼被别的鱼一口口吃掉了。

00:11:23.740 --> 00:11:30.620
And it's sort of like everyone has a theory about what it means, about the futility of life or it's a religious metaphor of whatever it is.
这就好像每个人对它都有一个理论，关于生命的虚无，或者这是一个宗教隐喻，诸如此类。

00:11:30.620 --> 00:11:37.230
And he just sort of, he had some quote that was like, the man is just a man, the fish is just a fish.
然后他差不多，他有这样一段话，大意是：人就是个人，鱼就是条鱼。

00:11:37.230 --> 00:11:39.389
The ocean is just a cigar.
海洋就是根雪茄。

00:11:39.389 --> 00:11:41.230
The ocean is just the ocean.
海洋就是海洋。

00:11:41.230 --> 00:11:43.549
I wrote this story about it, old fisherman.
我写了这个关于老渔夫的故事。

00:11:43.549 --> 00:11:46.309
He catches a fish, he tries to get it back to land and he can't.
他抓到一条鱼，他试图把它带回岸上，但他没能做到。

00:11:46.309 --> 00:11:51.750
And he's of course being a little tongue in cheek when he says that Hemingway is, because of course it meant something more to him.
当然，海明威说这话时有点半开玩笑的意味，因为对维来说，这显然有着更深层的含义。

00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:53.029
Everything means something more.
每件事都有更深层的含义。

00:11:53.029 --> 00:12:00.429
Anything you take that much time to dig that deep and write, whether it's how much mother or earnest Hemingway, it does have a deeper meaning.
任何你花那么多时间去深入挖掘并写下来的东西，无论是莫德还是厄尼斯特·海明威的作品，确实都有更深的意义。

00:12:00.429 --> 00:12:03.309
But I think his point there is, I don't always know what I'm writing.
但我认为他的重点在于：我并不总是知道自己在写什么。

00:12:03.309 --> 00:12:04.669
Shut up, just let me write.
闭嘴，让我写就行了。

00:12:04.669 --> 00:12:07.110
Let me just fucking follow some of these ideas.
就让我他妈的顺着这些想法写下去吧。

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:08.389
Let's not overthink it.
别想太多。

00:12:08.389 --> 00:12:10.549
Let's see what comes out and honor that.
看看会写出什么来，并顺其自然。

00:12:10.549 --> 00:12:14.269
Well, it's also kind of like, his job is to write that story.
嗯，这也有点像，他的工作是写出那个故事。

00:12:14.269 --> 00:12:20.710
Our job is to obsess over it, pick it apart, find what the metaphor is related to our own lives.
我们的工作则是对其着迷、剖析，找出与我们自己的生活相关的隐喻。

00:12:20.710 --> 00:12:28.110
It also kind of reminds me of, when John Stewart was at the height of his kind of daily show fame during the Bush years.
这也让我想起，当年乔恩·斯图尔特在小布什时期他的《每日秀》声望达到顶峰时的情况。

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:36.110
The more he insisted that he was just a comedian and not to be taken seriously, the more people took him seriously as a journalist.
他越是坚称自己只是个喜剧演员、不应该被严肃对待，人们就越是把他当作一个严肃的记者来对待。

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:43.590
He couldn't, the more he tried to kind of pull himself off that pedestal, the more people wanted to prop him up on it.
他越是试图把自己从那个神坛上拉下来，人们就越是想把他高高捧起。

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:46.809
Stanley Kubrick also prickly, also like.
斯坦利·库布里克同样尖锐，也总是……

00:12:46.809 --> 00:12:47.889
I got to be doing a new question.
我得问个新问题了。

00:12:47.889 --> 00:12:49.389
Stanley Kubrick has a question.
斯坦利·库布里克有个问题。

00:12:51.149 --> 00:12:55.710
He obviously was always, obviously constantly asked, especially about 2001, what does it mean to us in that?
显然，大家总是不断地问他问题，尤其是关于《2001太空漫游》的，这对我们意味着什么？

00:12:55.710 --> 00:13:00.070
And I believe it's him who said, if I knew it, it meant I wouldn't have to make it.
我记得正是他说过：如果我知道它意味着什么，我就不必把它拍出来了。

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:01.149
Well, that's it, right?
嗯，就是这样，对吧？

00:13:01.149 --> 00:13:04.429
They say the best art is asking questions, not answering them.
他们说最好的艺术是提出问题，而不是回答问题。

00:13:04.429 --> 00:13:06.389
And I think that that's true.
我认为这是真的。

00:13:06.389 --> 00:13:08.669
That's true because there aren't answers.
之所以它是真的，是因为根本没有标准答案。

00:13:08.669 --> 00:13:11.230
There are questions and there are attempts at answers.
只有问题以及对答案的尝试。

00:13:11.230 --> 00:13:20.429
And the attempts at answers are art and sometimes the art has several answers baked in or guesses or better, maybe those are better words for it.
对答案的尝试就是艺术，有时艺术本身就孕育着好几个答案、猜测，或者说得更好听点，或许这些才是更贴切的词。

00:13:20.429 --> 00:13:31.509
But I think honoring the mystery of that and then also acknowledging that there can be more rhyme and reason to that mystery than you think.
但我认为，既要尊重这种神秘感，同时也要承认，这种神秘感背后其实可以比你想象的更有逻辑和理路。

00:13:31.509 --> 00:13:38.710
You can be creating, you can create things have a structure that you see later, I think, too, which is interesting.
我认为你也可以在创作时，构建出某种你后来才看出来的结构，这也很有趣。

00:13:38.710 --> 00:13:46.710
Another couple of questions that I want to give an umbrella to, which is, I'll ask them and I'll explain.
另外还有几个我想用一个大纲把它们统筹起来的问题，我先提出来再解释。

00:13:46.710 --> 00:13:52.750
Kaylee Popmama says, was it hard to let Victoria go as a character at this point?
凯莉·波普玛玛问：在这个节点上，作为一个角色，要放手维多利亚是不是很难？

00:13:52.750 --> 00:13:55.470
She's so fantastic and they're me cute, it's so much fun.
她太棒了，而且他们的初次相遇（meet-cute）也太有趣了。

00:13:55.470 --> 00:14:03.110
And then the David Rob asks, how long did it take before you realized it would be fun to have Marshall and Barney work together?
然后大卫·罗伯问：你花了多久才意识到让马歇尔和巴尼一起工作会很有趣？

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:12.730
To expand that out, if I could, a little bit, to both of you as writers, when you make a, especially on a television show, I guess, well, movies also.
如果可以的话，我想把这个问题稍微扩展一下，抛给你们两位编剧：当你们做出一个决定时，特别是在电视剧里，我想，电影也是如此。

00:14:12.730 --> 00:14:17.250
When you make a small decision on a Thursday in the writer's room, hey, let's have them work together.
当你在周四的编剧室里做出了一个微小的决定——“嘿，让他们一起工作吧”。

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:22.529
Like, there's the back of your brain that has to be going, hold on, is that?
比如，你脑海深处肯定在想：等等，这样行吗？

00:14:22.529 --> 00:14:25.210
We have to, that's now true.
我们必须……这下就成既定事实了。

00:14:25.210 --> 00:14:30.889
So now we have months, maybe years, that we just have to accept that we made this one decision here on a Tuesday.
所以接下来我们得花好几个月，甚至好几年，去接受我们周二在这里做出的这个决定。

00:14:30.889 --> 00:14:38.250
Josh, you for writing films, you make one little decision now that stays, well, now you've got a couple of years.
乔什，你写电影也是如此，你现在做一个小决定，它就会留下来，好吧，现在你得花上好几年。

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:42.009
So you're going to make this film, you're going to post this film, you're going to work with this film.
所以你要拍这部电影，你要后期制作这部电影，你要为这部电影付出心血。

00:14:42.009 --> 00:14:51.409
And, you know, we've collaborated on some things and when you come up, when you know something is right, you're always open to more stuff to happen to you later.
而且，你知道的，我们曾就某些事情进行过合作，当你遇到、当你得知某个点子是正确的时候，你总是会敞开心扉，迎接后续更多可能会发生的事情。

00:14:51.409 --> 00:14:55.049
But you just had this, no, this is right now, this is where we're going.
但你当时只是觉得：不，这个是对的，这就是我们要走的方向。

00:14:55.049 --> 00:15:05.330
So I want to expand these questions out to short, tell me about Victoria and her leaving and talking about Marshall and Barney, but can you describe the moment of, yep, we got it.
所以我想把这些问题扩展开来：简单聊聊维多利亚和她的离开，以及马歇尔和巴尼，但是你能描述一下那个“没错，我们搞定了，这就是我们接下来要做的”那一刻吗？

00:15:05.330 --> 00:15:06.850
This is what we're going to do now.
就是我们要走的方向。

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:10.970
Well, two quick answers because those are just easy to answer quickly.
嗯，那就简单回答两个，因为这些问题很容易快速作答。

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:14.330
I definitely wish we had more Victoria before she went away.
我绝对希望在维多利亚离开之前，我们能有更多她的戏份。

00:15:14.330 --> 00:15:18.289
She was so great, but you plan things out ahead of time, right?
她太棒了，但你得提前把事情规划好，对吧？

00:15:18.289 --> 00:15:19.889
And she turned out to be so delightful.
而且她表现得如此讨人喜欢。

00:15:19.889 --> 00:15:24.939
When I watched the episode last night, or before our main episode that came out Monday about it.
昨晚当我重看那一集时，或者在看周一播出的关于它的主剧集之前。

00:15:24.939 --> 00:15:36.406
I will say, I found myself wishing I wish this was one of those 24 episode seasons because we were 22 episodes this season and then other seasons when we were a bigger hit, they would give us two extra episodes for 24.
我得说，我发现自己希望……我希望这是那种24集一季的剧，因为我们那一季只有22集，而在其他我们更受欢迎的季度，他们会给我们多加2集，凑够24集。

00:15:36.406 --> 00:15:41.140
If we'd had two more, I would have had two more fun, I think Carter would agree with this.
如果我们多两集，我想卡特也会同意我的看法，我们就能多拍两集他们在一起的有趣剧情。

00:15:41.140 --> 00:15:45.348
I thought we would have had two more fun episodes where they were just together.
让维多利亚多待一会儿，因为这只会对后面的剧情有帮助，对吧？

00:15:45.348 --> 00:15:48.610
We're a Victorian tad because it only helps what comes, right?
如果他们在一起的时间更多，而且在我们不得不把她送去德国之前，能有更多欢乐时光的话，那么后来的戏剧冲突就会表现得更好。

00:15:48.610 --> 00:15:58.370
If they're more together and the drama later plays better if there was more fun to be we had before we had to send her up to Germany, but it just sort of that's what we booked the actress for.
但这就是我们跟那位女演员签约的时长。

00:15:58.370 --> 00:15:59.450
That's how we planned it out.
这就是我们的规划方式。

00:15:59.450 --> 00:16:02.090
That's how many episodes we had, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.
我们当时只有那么多集，诸如此类，诸如此类。

00:16:02.090 --> 00:16:06.769
But yes, I did find myself thinking, we could have had a couple more fun ones just with her hanging out, because she's so great.
但是的，我确实发现自己在想，我们本可以让她多待一会儿，多拍几场有趣的戏，因为她实在是太棒了。

00:16:06.769 --> 00:16:20.250
And then Barney and Marshall, I think what I like about that storyline in Cupcake is it's another kind of question of your late 20s, which is not related to dating or love.
然后是巴尼和马歇尔，我觉得我在《纸杯蛋糕》那一集里喜欢那条故事线的原因是，它提出了你在二十几岁末期会遇到的另一种人生问题，而这与约会或爱情无关。

00:16:20.250 --> 00:16:23.769
It's what are my principles in my career?
这就是：我在职业生涯中的原则是什么？

00:16:23.769 --> 00:16:25.769
What are my principles in my career?
我在职业生涯中的原则是什么？

00:16:25.769 --> 00:16:35.850
Am I going to try to be the good guy and do the right thing and make no money and work for the NRDC and try to save the environment and not sell out and become a corporate lawyer?
我是要努力做一个好人、做正确的事、赚不到什么钱、为自然资源保护委员会（NRDC）工作、试图拯救环境、绝不向利益妥协去当个公司大律师呢？

00:16:35.850 --> 00:16:40.570
Or am I going to be seduced by wanting to make some money because now I'm an adult and I want to make money?
还是会被赚钱的欲望所诱惑，因为现在我是个成年人了，我想赚钱？

00:16:40.570 --> 00:16:46.009
And in this episode, Marshall only get deeply in debt due to wardrobe choices.
而在这一集里，马歇尔仅仅因为置装选择就陷入了深深的债务危机。

00:16:46.009 --> 00:17:08.329
And I love that we started playing with that idea of getting seduced off your intended path as you get later into your 20s and you're really an adult now, and exploring that Barney would be the guy saying, yes, come towards me, come towards the suits, come towards the money, and Marshall has these high-minded lofty ideals and the clash of those two things.
我特别喜欢我们开始探讨这样一种想法：当你步入二十几岁后期、真正成为一个成年人时，你会被诱惑偏离原定的轨道，并且我们去探索了巴尼会是那个说“来吧，向我靠拢，向西装靠拢，向金钱靠拢”的人，而马歇尔则有着崇高宏大的理想，这两种东西产生了碰撞。

00:17:08.329 --> 00:17:28.150
And Barney is such a great seducer, not just of women, but of Marshall off of this path of the light side of things, and I really liked that, and yes, we knew we wanted Barney to be that voice of seduction, and that's sort of the challenge to Marshall's commitment to his high-minded ideals.
巴尼是一个如此出色的“诱惑者”，他不仅能诱惑女人，还能诱惑马歇尔偏离那条光明正大的道路，我真的很喜欢这一点。是的，我们知道我们想要巴尼成为那个诱惑之声，而这正是对马歇尔坚守崇高理想的一种挑战。

00:17:28.150 --> 00:17:50.549
It also reminds you something, and I think I'd mentioned this earlier, but you was either Greg Malins or Jonathan Groff, or someone who had a lot of years in writers' rooms, and he said, you know a show is really kind of popping and has some heat to it when you You can take any two characters, put them in a scene, and you can kind of, you can play with what happens.
这也提醒了你一件事，我想我之前提到过，要么是格雷格·马林斯（Greg Malins），要么是乔纳森·格罗夫（Jonathan Groff），或者某个在编剧室里呆了很多年的人，他说过，你知道一部剧什么时候真正开始火爆、有热度，那就是你可以把任意两个角色放在一个场景里，然后你可以去拿捏、去演绎接下来会发生什么。

00:17:50.549 --> 00:17:55.589
There's a dynamic ad play that you're like, and I think you guys were still exercising.
有一种动态广告的玩法，你会觉得，而且我记得你们当时还在练习。

00:17:55.589 --> 00:17:57.670
You're like, oh, we have this trick up our sleeve.
你会觉得，哦，我们还有这一手绝活。

00:17:57.670 --> 00:17:58.670
We could do this too.
我们也可以这么做。

00:17:58.670 --> 00:17:59.670
Yeah.
对。

00:17:59.670 --> 00:18:07.829
And I think there was like, there was a funny chemistry between Neil and Jason that was different than the chemistry between me and Neil, different than the chemistry between me and Jason.
而且我觉得尼尔和杰森之间有一种有趣的化学反应，它不同于我和尼尔之间的化学反应，也不同于我和杰森之间的化学反应。

00:18:07.829 --> 00:18:12.029
And it was just another speed on the bike that you were like, oh, we have this too.
这就像是自行车上的另一个档位，你会觉得，哦，我们连这个也有。

00:18:12.029 --> 00:18:51.279
You're watching us find it in real time in this episode, yeah, yeah regarding Alex bigger question I do think sometimes and I try not to get to in my head about this because Sometimes a decision you can make on like a Thursday afternoon in your script will prove to be quite consequential and it can actually lead you down a path that is You know, it's gonna be hard to unwind and sometimes like I always send my scripts to Alec because he's such a brilliant guy around structure and stuff, and sometimes he'll recommend something that I'm like, but that would require re-writing 75% of the script, you know?
你们正在这一集中实时看到我们发现这一点，对，对，关于亚历克斯的那个更大的问题，我确实有时会这么想，我尽量不让自己在这方面想得太复杂，因为有时候你在周四下午在剧本里做出的一个决定，事后证明会产生很大的影响，它实际上可能会把你带上一条……你知道的，很难回头的路，有时候就像我总是把剧本发给亚历克斯（Alec），因为他在结构之类的事情上是个天才，有时他会推荐一些东西，让我觉得，但这需要重写75%的剧本，你知道吗？

00:18:51.279 --> 00:18:55.927
So I'll have to tell him sometimes, like, I like this, so don't kill it.
所以我有时不得不告诉他，比如，我很喜欢这个，所以别把它删了。

00:18:55.927 --> 00:19:09.289
But there is a thing where I think you have a little more leeway in series television to kind of explore little crevices and maybe not go so deep into them, but we're just Yeah, dipping over here in a movie.
但有这样一种情况，我觉得在系列电视剧中，你会有更多的余地去探索一些小细节，可能不需要挖得那么深，我们只是……对，在这里稍微涉猎一下，而在电影里。

00:19:09.289 --> 00:19:14.529
It's so much tighter, you know, 90 minutes, 120 minutes, like you've really got to have things become very consequential.
节奏要紧凑得多，你知道的，90分钟，120分钟，你真的必须让事情产生非常重大的影响。

00:19:14.529 --> 00:19:17.410
If you plant something in the first act, it's got to go off in the third.
如果你在第一幕埋下一个伏笔，它就必须在第三幕爆发。

00:19:17.410 --> 00:19:22.130
We have a big canvas to paint on it with 22 episodes a year for multiple years.
我们有一张巨大的画布可以挥洒，一年22集，连续多年。

00:19:22.130 --> 00:19:23.690
That is a big canvas to try stuff.
这是一个尝试各种新事物的巨大画布。

00:19:23.690 --> 00:19:25.250
It's very different than a movie.
这和电影非常不同。

00:19:25.250 --> 00:19:27.289
Boy, a movie is not a lot of real estate.
天哪，电影可没有那么大的发挥空间。

00:19:27.289 --> 00:19:28.690
No.
没有。

00:19:28.690 --> 00:19:37.670
But you could also see that, you know, even though you wish Ashley could have been on more episodes than I do too, like, I don't know, She was, I don't know, I think 16 total.
但你也能看到，你知道的，尽管你希望阿什利（Ashley）能多拍几集，我也希望，我不知道，她拍了，我不知道，我想总共16集吧。

00:19:37.670 --> 00:19:41.009
Like, if she hadn't been so good, she would have been on four.
如果她不是那么优秀，她可能只演4集。

00:19:41.009 --> 00:19:41.849
You know?
你知道吗？

00:19:41.849 --> 00:19:42.690
Right, right.
对，对。

00:19:42.690 --> 00:19:43.529
Yeah.
对。

00:19:43.529 --> 00:19:47.170
And I maintain that this was not an easy show to guest star on.
而且我坚持认为，要在客串这个剧并不容易。

00:19:47.170 --> 00:20:00.009
The five of us were like so particular, the energy of the five of us at that booth was very loud and strong and for people who could really get in there and mix it up with us, I was always impressed with it and Ashley was certainly one of those.
我们五个人非常有特点，我们在那个片场的五人组合能量非常嘈杂而强烈，对于那些能够真正加入进来并与我们融为一体的人，我一直对他们印象深刻，阿什利绝对是其中之一。

00:20:00.009 --> 00:20:00.970
Absolutely.
绝对是的。

00:20:00.970 --> 00:20:02.529
She deserved so much credit.
她理应获得极高的赞誉。

00:20:02.529 --> 00:20:34.690
First of all, Josh, I will tell you here, I was very honored when you asked for my help on anything and I really enjoy the dynamic of my my structure monkey Architect Ted Mosby brain that part of the Ted that part has brain doing that and then Finding your your heart and your your art and and and me knowing to go all right don't push on that anymore That's that's coming from a deep place from him But yeah, I mean, like the smallest details is Victoria, does she make Bavarian pretzels or cupcakes?
首先，乔什，我要在这里对你说，当你在任何事情上请求我的帮助时，我都感到非常荣幸，我真的很喜欢我的结构狂热分子、建筑师泰德·莫斯伯格（Ted Mosby）大脑的这种动态，泰德的那一部分大脑在做这件事，然后去寻找你的内心情感和你的艺术，而我知道该说“好了，不要再强求那个了，那是他内心深处流露出来的东西”……但是，是的，我是说，就像最微小的细节是维多利亚，她是做巴伐利亚蝴蝶圈还是纸杯蛋糕？

00:20:34.690 --> 00:20:41.289
I mean, you just choose one thing, all of a sudden cupcakes become an important part of her story.
我的意思是，你只要选择一件事，突然之间纸杯蛋糕就成了她故事中的重要部分。

00:20:41.289 --> 00:20:47.170
Just anything is going to, hopefully, not just, it won't happen to reverberate.
任何事情都有望产生回响，不仅仅是……它不会只是无缘无故地产生回响。

00:20:47.170 --> 00:20:49.250
This is storytelling, this is art with a through line.
这就是讲故事，这就是带有贯穿主线的艺术。

00:20:49.250 --> 00:20:50.690
It should reverberate.
它应该产生回响。

00:20:50.690 --> 00:20:53.450
And just that moment of going, we're doing this thing.
仅仅是那种“我们在做这件事”的时刻，

00:20:53.450 --> 00:21:00.829
and a mixture of, let's see where it goes, versus, oh, and let's plan where it goes, is also the fun part.
以及“让我们看看它会走向何方”与“让我们规划它会走向何方”的结合，这也是有趣的部分。

00:21:00.829 --> 00:21:01.670
Yeah.
对。

00:21:01.670 --> 00:21:06.269
Well, it's also like, you as a writer, you go, okay, he's an architect, cool, architect's a cool job.
嗯，这也像是，作为一个编剧，你会想，好吧，他是个建筑师，酷，建筑师是个很酷的职业。

00:21:06.269 --> 00:21:08.069
And then you're like, wait, architect's a metaphor.
然后你会想，等等，建筑师是一个隐喻。

00:21:08.069 --> 00:21:09.430
He's trying to build his life.
他在试图构建自己的生活。

00:21:09.430 --> 00:21:12.269
You know, she's a baker, she's making these confections.
你知道的，她是个面包师，她在制作这些甜点。

00:21:12.269 --> 00:21:13.509
Oh, she's sweet.
哦，她很甜美。

00:21:13.509 --> 00:21:15.329
She's also building something.
她也在构建某些东西。

00:21:15.329 --> 00:21:16.869
They're trying to build this thing together.
他们正试图一起构建这个东西。

00:21:16.869 --> 00:21:17.869
It's not quite working.
但进展得不太顺利。

00:21:17.869 --> 00:21:25.190
Like, you just start making all these connections, you know, based on these maybe haphazard, like just like, sure, she could be a baker.
就像，你开始建立所有这些联系，你知道的，基于这些可能是随意的、就像是“好吧，她可以是个面包师”的想法。

00:21:25.190 --> 00:21:29.109
Yeah, but then you look back at it and you go, wait, that added up in ways that worked.
是的，但随后你回过头去看，你会想，等等，这些以一种行之有效的方式汇聚在了一起。

00:21:29.109 --> 00:21:30.950
You know, even if some of it was haphazard.
你知道的，即使其中有些是随意的。

00:21:30.950 --> 00:21:31.789
Yeah.
对。

00:21:31.789 --> 00:21:41.430
So a great question from Brian Narr, who points out how much of the whole series is in cupcake.
所以，布莱恩·纳尔（Brian Narr）提出了一个很好的问题，他指出整部剧有多少内容都围绕着纸杯蛋糕。

00:21:41.630 --> 00:21:47.470
And Josh, you talk about this a lot on the podcast of how so many of these just kind of show everything.
乔什，你在播客中经常谈到这一点，谈到其中许多内容是如何展现一切的。

00:21:47.470 --> 00:21:50.390
Cupcake feels like the classic Hemium dilemma.
纸杯蛋糕感觉像是经典的赫米姆（Hemium）困境。

00:21:50.390 --> 00:21:55.390
Timing, ambition, love, and missed chances.
时机、野心、爱情和错失的机遇。

00:21:56.190 --> 00:22:03.230
Timing, ambition, love, and missed chances was that part of what you saw as the emotional core of the series.
时机、野心、爱情和错失的机遇，这是否是你所看到的剧集情感核心的一部分？

00:22:03.230 --> 00:22:06.829
Yeah, I mean, that's a big time.
是的，我的意思是，这是一个很大的时代。

00:22:06.829 --> 00:22:07.990
It's a lot of it.
这占了很大一部分。

00:22:07.990 --> 00:22:09.309
Yeah, it's all of it.
是的，这就是全部。

00:22:09.309 --> 00:22:13.349
These are people in their late 20s, and that was one of my favorite things with rewatching the episode.
这些人正处于二十岁快结束的年纪，这也是我重温这一集时最喜欢的点之一。

00:22:13.349 --> 00:22:33.309
The money dilemma, the career dilemma for Marshall, the money dilemma for getting married, It kind of branched out of like, what's going to happen with Ted and this woman and became this like, nexus, this like, this seven layer salad, if you will, of like, so many different late 20s dilemmas.
金钱的困境，马歇尔的职业困境，结婚的金钱困境，它有点像从“泰德和这个女人会发生什么”延伸开来，变成了一个枢纽，一个怎么说呢，七层沙拉一样的存在，包含了如此多不同二十岁末期的困境。

00:22:33.309 --> 00:22:35.589
People trying to figure out who they are as adults.
人们在试图弄清楚自己作为成年人究竟是谁。

00:22:35.589 --> 00:22:40.390
There's a lot of ways that shows up in life, and it's not all dating and love.
这种情况在生活中有很多表现形式，并非只有约会和爱情。

00:22:40.390 --> 00:22:46.230
And it's also, we've talked about this, but like, there's a lot of like, micro macro stuff going on.
而且正如我们所讨论过的，这里面有很多微观和宏观的东西在发生。

00:22:46.230 --> 00:22:48.589
it's almost like the whole show is a holograph.
这几乎就像整部剧是一个全息图。

00:22:48.589 --> 00:22:53.589
And the point of a holograph is like, you pull out any part and the whole is contained in the part.
全息图的意义在于，你抽出任何一部分，整体都包含在那一部分中。

00:22:53.589 --> 00:23:00.710
And I feel like you could take any episode of How I Met Your Mother, or a certain maybe strain of episodes.
我觉得你可以挑选《老爸老妈浪漫史》的任何一集，或者某种特定类型的剧集。

00:23:00.710 --> 00:23:05.630
And you're like, the whole show, the whole series is in the pilot.
然后你会觉得，整部剧，整个系列都在试播集中。

00:23:05.630 --> 00:23:08.509
The whole series is in drum roll, please.
整个系列都在《击鼓传花，拜托》（Drumroll, Please）里。

00:23:08.509 --> 00:23:25.069
The whole series, any time you have like a mystery, a kind of what Aristotle calls a recognition and reversal at the end, you know, it defies your expectations, it hits those four themes that this writer wrote in.
整个系列，每当你有一个悬念，一种亚里士多德所说的在结尾处的认出与反转，你知道的，它打破了你的预期，它击中了这位编剧所融入的那四个主题。

00:23:26.750 --> 00:23:35.549
There are just these episodes where there's a DNA to the show, and the strain of it runs through the entire thing.
总有一些剧集拥有这部剧的DNA，而这种血脉贯穿了始终。

00:23:35.549 --> 00:23:39.150
Well, thanks again for hanging with us for general questions.
好吧，再次感谢大家抽出时间听我们回答常规问题。

00:23:39.150 --> 00:23:40.710
General questions?
常规问题？

00:23:40.710 --> 00:23:41.549
That was a good one.
这个问题不错。

00:23:41.549 --> 00:23:42.710
That was good, that was solid.
挺好的，很扎实。

